WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.033
Idea. A small bakery pays six hundred dollars rent each month, however many cakes it sells. On a break-even chart, this fixed cost is a flat line.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:21.105
Each cake also needs two dollars of ingredients. Add these variable costs to the rent and you get total costs, a line that starts at six hundred and rises.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:28.643
Each cake sells for five dollars. Total revenue starts at zero, because no sales means no money in.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:38.174
Where total revenue crosses total costs is break-even: two hundred cakes. Below it, the bakery makes a loss. Above it, a profit.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:50.263
Method. Some questions ask what happens when something changes. Higher fixed costs or higher variable costs raise break-even. A higher selling price lowers it.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:59.693
Break-even is useful. It shows how many units must be sold, lets managers ask what if questions, and supports a loan application.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:09.495
But it has limits. It assumes everything made is sold, that costs and revenue rise in straight lines, and its figures are only predictions.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:24.347
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2025, Paper two. Explain one limitation of a break-even chart. It is worth three marks: one limitation, then two linked steps.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.413
Mark one: the limitation. A break-even chart assumes every unit made is sold.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:39.905
Mark two: why that is unrealistic. Samsung may not sell all its televisions, for example if a rival launches a cheaper model.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:52.018
Mark three: the consequence. Real revenue is lower than the chart shows, so Samsung may need more sales to break even and could plan output on figures that are too optimistic.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:02:02.929
Common mistake. A common mistake is putting break-even where revenue meets the fixed cost line. Break-even is where revenue meets total costs.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.440
Another is getting the direction wrong. A higher price does not raise break-even. It lowers it.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:27.701
Your turn. Now you try. A bike repair shop has fixed costs of one thousand two hundred dollars a month and spends eight dollars on parts per repair. It raises its price from twenty to twenty-five dollars. Does break-even rise or fall? Pause and decide.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:43.335
Answer. Break-even falls. Before, each repair contributes twelve dollars, so it needs one hundred repairs. After, each contributes seventeen dollars, so seventy point six, rounded up to seventy-one repairs.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:54.245
For Explain and Analyse answers, name the use or the limit, link it to the business, then say how it affects its decision. Now try the practice questions.
